unks/14mrh2-p_w84d.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/2nzzixgj4wils.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/0taliq9wft957.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/3ppkpjc789k8m.js\\\"],\\\"SpeakButton\\\"]\\n22:I[5500,[\\\"/_next/static/chunks/3vsl20q6wdw12.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/0abm728svpw4d.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/14mrh2-p_w84d.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/2nzzixgj4wils.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/0taliq9wft957.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/3ppkpjc789k8m.js\\\"],\\\"Image\\\"]\\n21:T31a5,\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"Shri Shankhala Parshwanath, Shri Gadaliya Parshwanath and Shri Sherisa Parshwanath. 52. Shri Shankhala Parshwanath. Main Deity: The Mulanayak is Lord Shantinath. To the right of Lord Shantinath stands a tall, black-hued idol of Lord Shri Shankhala Parshwanath in the padmasana posture, 27 inches tall and 19 inches wide. Above the idol's head is a canopy of seven serpent hoods.. Location: This temple is at the center of the village of Shankhalpur, in the Patidar Mahajan neighborhood.. History: Sambhalpur was an ancient and highly prosperous town. It was founded by King Lakhman and was named Salakhanpur. Between the 14th and 17th centuries, the Jain community held sway over this town. They were wealthy, devout, and prosperous. During this period, two beautiful Jain temples - of Lord Shantinath and Lord Parshwanath - were built here by Pethadshah, the minister of Mandavgadh. The temples were destroyed (the reason is unknown), but the idols of the Lords were hidden away to protect them. In V.S. 1848, a robber stole the two Tirthankara idols from Ratej and brought them to Shankhalpur. A Jain devotee purchased these idols from him. He kept one idol with himself and gave the other to the Jain Sangh of Tuvad village. He purchased an old, ruined site to build a temple. While digging at that spot, they discovered, buried in the ground, several idols of Tirthankaras and deities, temple spires, and the ruins of many other destroyed temples. From here, many idols were taken to Kadamgiri Tirth and to several other temples as well. This shows that this village once had many Jain temples that were destroyed, their idols hidden away for safekeeping. A new temple with three spires was built, and the idol of Lord Shantinath was installed as the Mulanayak on the 8th day of the dark half of the month of Jeth in V.S. 1905. Every year on this day, the temple flag is hoisted.. The ancient and powerful idol of Lord Parshwanath installed here is known, after the village, as \\\"Shankhala Parshwanath.\\\" The temple is highly miraculous and powerful. In the basement of the temple is an idol of Navkhanda Parshwanath, which is extremely miraculous and magnificent. Many times, the idol of Navkhanda Parshwanath has been found smeared with saffron paste on its own. Often, when a devotee enters the temple, a beautiful fragrance fills the air, and upon investigation it is found that a shower of fragrant flowers has fallen before the idol. Sometimes, in the evening, the aarti bell rings by itself, and its sound can be heard from far away. Once, a square silver coin was found near the idol of Navkhanda Parshwanath. Such miracles continue to occur here. For many years, an \\\"akhand deepak\\\" (perpetual lamp) has been kept burning before the idol of Navkhanda Parshwanath. There are more than 25 idols of Tirthankaras here, all ancient, beautiful, and attractive.. Directions: Shankhalpur is 2 km from Becharaji railway station. Shankhalpur has recently become a flag station. There are two upashrayas here. Ratej, Shankheshwar, and Bhoyani Tirth are all nearby.. Scripture References: Shankhala Parshwanath is mentioned in \\\"Tirthmala,\\\" \\\"108 Nam Garbhit Shri Godiji Parshwanath Stavan,\\\" \\\"Guruvali,\\\" \\\"108 Nam Garbhit Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,\\\" \\\"Kochar Vihari Ras,\\\" and others. An idol of Shankhala Parshwanath is found at Jiravala Tirth and at the Kalikund Parshwanath Temple in Santacruz, Mumbai.. Hymn of Praise: O Lord, your smiling face - beholding it, all my sorrows melt away; you alone grant the imperishable state, and through you my cycle of rebirth is cut short; O Lord, grant me a place in your vast realm of liberation. \\\"With loving devotion I bow to Shri Shankhala Parshwa.\\\". Chanting Mantra: \\\"Om Hrim Arham Shri Shankhala Parshwanathaya Namah\\\". Trust: Shri Shankhala Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Patidar Mahajan ni Gali, Post: Shankhalpur, Station: Becharaji, Taluka: Chanasma, District: Mehsana, State: Gujarat - 384210, India. Phone: 02734-284408.. Hymn of Praise to Lord Parshwanath: I remember Shri Shankheshwar Parshwaprabhu, and meditate on the infinite Arihants; I drink the nectar of the Jina's teachings, and may the guardian goddess remove all obstacles. Rajendra Kumar Jain - Jai Jinendra.. 53. Shri Gadaliya Parshwanath. Address: Mu. Po. Mandal, Taluka: Viramgam - 382130, District: Ahmedabad. Main Deity: A beautiful white idol of Lord Shri Gadaliya Parshwanath, 10 inches tall and 6.25 inches wide, with an elegant parikar, in the padmasana posture. Above the idol's head is a canopy of seven serpent hoods.. Location: This temple is at the center of the village of Mandal, in Gandhivas.. History: Mandal is an ancient village of great historical significance. It is the birthplace of the great ministers Vastupal and Tejpal, renowned for their contributions to Jainism. They had numerous temples built across Gujarat. The world-famous Dilwara temples near Mount Abu were also built by them. They led as many as twelve and a half chhari-palit (rule-observing) pilgrimages to the great Shatrunjay Tirth. In ancient times this place was known as one of the most famous and sacred Jain pilgrimage sites, called Shri Gadaliya Parshwanath.. This form of Parshwanath is referred to by various names in ancient hymns - \\\"Galiya Parshwanath,\\\" \\\"Garaliya Parshwanath\\\" - and is also referenced as \\\"Gadar,\\\" \\\"Gadari,\\\" and \\\"Garadiya Parshwanath.\\\" Because the padmasana seat (\\\"gaadi\\\") of this idol is shaped like a \\\"galli\\\" (cart), the Lord became renowned by the name Gadaliya Parshwanath.. The village of Mandal is also the birthplace of Vastupal and Tejpal. Because this idol is believed to act like the Garudi Mantra in removing the poison of ailments and afflictions, it is also famous under the name Garadiya Parshwanath. The glory of this ancient, appealing, powerful, and miraculous idol is boundless. This idol dates from the era of King Samprati. The present temple was built around V.S. 1875. Every year, the temple flag is hoisted on the fifth day of the bright half of the month of Magh.. Directions: The nearest railway station here is Viramgam, 25 kilometers away. Shankheshwar Parshwanath is 35 kilometers from here. Dharamshala and bhojanshala facilities are available here, but prior notice is required for the bhojanshala.. Scripture References: Shri Gadaliya Parshwanath is mentioned in the \\\"365 Shri Parshvajin Nammala,\\\" composed by Shri Premvijayji in V.S. 1665, and in the \\\"Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,\\\" composed by the poet Naysundar on Tuesday, the ninth day of the dark half of Aso, in Samvat 1656.. Hymn of Praise: O Lord of Mandal, illumine my soul; with the Garudi mantra, dispel the poison of delusion (moha); turn back my cart that travels the path to a lower rebirth. \\\"With loving devotion I bow to Shri Gadaliya Parshwa.\\\". Chanting Mantra: \\\"Om Hrim Arham Shri Gadaliya Parshwanathaya Namah\\\". Trust: Shri Gadaliya Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Post: Mandal, Taluka Viramgam, District: Ahmedabad. Mobile: 9898422903/7600584235.. The Ocean of Bliss, Love, and Compassion: The root foundation of world peace, the supreme practitioner of samadhi (spiritual equanimity), the great ocean of bliss, love, and compassion. Having destroyed the four ghati (obscuring) karmas, all Tirthankaras become embodiments of supreme light and become lords of the glory of the eight great prophetic marvels (ashta-mahapratiharya). When they walk upon the earth, nine golden lotuses, fresh as butter, appear beneath their lotus feet. Even the wind becomes favorable, granting them supreme comfort. Birds too circumambulate this great light. In service of this supreme sovereign of nature's majesty, all the seasons present themselves together at once. Before this magnificently powerful image, crores of gods and goddesses come to attend. Their arrival fills the very sky. Just as the majesty (aishwarya) of all Tirthankaras is equal, so too is the fullness of their virtues equal; yet, owing to the strength of his \\\"adeya nam karma\\\" (the karma of universal appeal), the twenty-third Lord of the Tirtha, Shri Parshwanath Swami, stands apart from the other Tirthankaras. Thus, in the scriptural texts (agamas), the epithet \\\"Purushadaniya\\\" (esteemed by all) is well known as applied to Lord Shri Parshwanath. When, out of ignorance, the ascetic Kamath was performing his fire-penance, Parshwakumar drew a half-burnt serpent out of the flames and, through the Navkar Mantra, granted it a wondrous state of peace (samadhi). It is for this very reason that the divine soul who became Dharanendra remains ever devoted to increasing the glory of his supreme benefactor, the Lord. It is believed that hundreds of female ascetics (sadhvis) of Shri Parshwanath's dispensation are now, in their form as goddesses, ever striving to further the glory of the Lord. Whatever the reason, one truth is clear - everyone's heart holds a special attraction toward Shri Parshwanathswami. In most of India's Jain temples, it is Shri Parshwanathswami who is enshrined as the Mulanayak. The oldest pilgrimage sites and idols, too, are mostly those of Lord Parshwanath. The idols of Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath, Shri Stambhan Parshwanath, Shri Charup Parshwanath, Shri Ajahara Parshwanath, and others are counted among the most ancient surviving idols. Even today, in hundreds of temples of Shri Parshwanath Prabhu across India, the sounds of divine dance, song, and music are heard at night. In many temples, miracles such as showers of nectar, nectar flowing from idols, showers of saffron, and the spreading of divine fragrance are often experienced.. There is but one Tirthankara Parshwanath, yet, in accordance with the varied powers manifest in his images enshrined at different places, various names for him have become current. Some of these names derive from the power (prabhav) associated with the image, while others derive from the sheer beauty (manoharita) of the idol. Various names of Shri Parshwanathswami are also found in numerous ancient texts, poems, tirtha-kalpas, chhands, ras, stotras, and stavans. More than a hundred pilgrimage sites of Parshwanathswami still exist today. The images, powers, and detailed histories of the 108 different forms of Lord Parshwanath are still available today. May this shield of the supreme practitioner of samadhi - who, through the elixir of equanimity, protects the soul from becoming tainted at every difficult juncture along the ten-birth journey to liberation - become a source of samadhi for all living beings.. 54. Shri Sherisa Parshwanath (beginning). Main Deity: A black-hued idol of Lord Shri Sherisa Parshwanath, about 165 cm tall, in the padmasana posture. This idol is carved from a single piece of stone.. Location: This temple is in the village of Sherisa.. History: This temple dates from the 12th century. There is a very interesting story behind the origin of this beautiful and delightful idol of Lord Sherisa Parshwanath. Beholding the natural beauty of the village of Sherisa, Acharya Shri Devendrasurishwarji was filled with great joy and felt that this place was well suited for spiritual endeavor. Through his divine knowledge, he perceived a massive stone lying buried underground in the village and thought that a beautiful idol of the Lord could be carved from that stone. He therefore worshipped the goddess Padmavati through a three-day fast (attham tap). The goddess Padmavati appeared before him and gave him a sign to have the idol made. Accordingly, a blind sculptor from the village of Soparak was summoned. After sunset, this blind sculptor began carving a beautiful idol from the stone that had been found underground. Before sunrise, in a single night, a captivating idol of Parshwanath was carved. That same night, the acharya, through his divine powers, resolved to bring four other idols of Tirthankaras from Ayodhya. Three idols reached the vicinity of Sherisa before sunrise, and one idol was installed in the village of Dharasenak. Twenty-four idols of Tirthankaras were brought near Sherisa, and Emperor Kumarpal too obtained an attractive idol for this temple. Acharya Shri Devendrasurishwarji installed all these idols in a vast temple. The idol carved by the blind sculptor came to be known as \\\"Sherisa Parshwanath.\\\" Since the Mulanayak idol of Parshwanath had been wobbling unsteadily, it also became known as \\\"Lodhan Parshwanath\\\" (\\\"the wobbling one\\\"). Through the acharya's divine powers, the idol ceased to wobble and became firmly steady. In the 13th century, the great minister Tejpal had two devkulikas (small shrines) built in this temple, one of which was consecrated with an idol of Lord Neminath along with its parikar (the account continues on the following pages).\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1b:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"a\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"https://goo.gl/maps/g3dypMPQFrtexa9U7\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"_blank\\\",\\\"rel\\\":\\\"noopener noreferrer\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-sm bg-maroon px-5 py-2.5 font-ui text-sm font-medium text-ivory hover:bg-maroon-deep transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":\\\"$8:2:props